===== README.md =====
     1	# Relux Proxy
     2	
     3	Relux Proxy currently ships as a native macOS menu bar app that manages an SSH
     4	dynamic SOCKS5 tunnel. It uses the system `/usr/bin/ssh`, so hosts, keys, jump
     5	hosts, and other options from `~/.ssh/config` continue to work.
     6	
     7	The repository also contains the approved specifications and implementation
     8	backlog for a future macOS/iOS system VPN over SSH. That VPN is not implemented
     9	yet. Start with [the specification index](.spec/README.md),
    10	[the current-state inventory](docs/current-state.md), and
    11	[the saved task-board workflow](docs/project-management.md).
    12	
    13	## Use
    14	
    15	1. Open Relux Proxy from Applications.
    16	2. Enter the SSH host (or config alias), account, and local SOCKS port.
    17	3. Choose **Connect**.
    18	4. In Firefox, open **Settings → Network Settings**, select **Manual proxy configuration**, and set:
    19	   - SOCKS Host: `127.0.0.1`
    20	   - Port: the port shown by Relux Proxy (`1080` by default)
    21	   - SOCKS v5
    22	   - Proxy DNS when using SOCKS v5
    23	
    24	The initial defaults are `administrator@relux` and `127.0.0.1:1080`.
    25	
    26	## Download
    27	
    28	The latest signed and notarized build always has the same asset name and URL
    29	(GitHub authentication is required while this repository is private):
    30	
    31	https://github.com/relux-works/relux-proxy/releases/latest/download/ReluxProxy.dmg
    32	
    33	Versioned DMGs and SHA-256 checksums are available on the GitHub Releases page.
    34	
    35	## Development
    36	
    37	Requirements: macOS 14 or newer and Xcode 15.3 or newer.
    38	
    39	```bash
    40	make test
    41	make app
    42	make run
    43	```
    44	
    45	The app is intentionally not sandboxed because it needs to launch the system SSH executable. Release bundles use the hardened runtime and are signed with the Relux Works Developer ID.
    46	
    47	Contributors must follow [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md). Planned VPN work is
    48	tracked in `.task-board/`; no VPN implementation should begin without explicit
    49	approval of the generated plan.
    50	
    51	## Release
    52	
    53	Push a semantic version tag to build, sign, notarize, staple, attest, and publish both versioned and stable-name DMGs:
    54	
    55	```bash
    56	git tag -s v0.1.0 -m "Relux Proxy v0.1.0"
    57	git push origin v0.1.0
    58	```
    59	
    60	The release workflow expects `MACOS_CERT_P12`, `ASC_KEY_ID`, `ASC_ISSUER_ID`, and `ASC_KEY_P8` repository secrets. `ASC_KEY_P8` and `MACOS_CERT_P12` are base64 encoded.

===== SECURITY.md =====
     1	# Security policy
     2	
     3	## Reporting a vulnerability
     4	
     5	Do not open a public issue containing a vulnerability, private key, host
     6	fingerprint, traffic capture, or signing credential. Report security concerns
     7	privately to the Relux Works repository maintainers through GitHub Security
     8	Advisories for `relux-works/relux-proxy`.
     9	
    10	Include the affected commit/version, platform and OS version, reproduction steps,
    11	impact, and only the minimum redacted logs needed. Do not include real user
    12	traffic or production credentials.
    13	
    14	## Supported state
    15	
    16	The released macOS SOCKS application is the only currently implemented product.
    17	The VPN architecture under `.spec/` is pre-implementation and has no supported
    18	binary yet. Security reports about design flaws in the specifications are still
    19	welcome and should reference the relevant document/decision.
    20	
    21	## Security invariants for planned work
    22	
    23	- SSH host-key verification cannot be disabled silently.
    24	- Private keys/passphrases stay in Keychain and out of logs/configuration files.
    25	- Ordinary DNS cannot fall back outside an advertised connected VPN mode.
    26	- Remote relay binaries are verified before execution and never require a public
    27	  listening port.
    28	- No private Apple APIs, utun descriptor discovery, or hidden entitlement bypass
    29	  is accepted.
    30	- VPN traffic payloads and destinations are not telemetry.
    31	
    32	See `.spec/security-privacy.md` for the full threat model and privacy contract.

===== CONTRIBUTING.md =====
     1	# Contributing
     2	
     3	## Before changing the product
     4	
     5	1. Read `.spec/README.md` and `docs/current-state.md`.
     6	2. Install the project-management tooling using `docs/project-management.md`.
     7	3. Find or create the board element before implementation.
     8	4. Use `task-board` for every `.task-board/` mutation; do not edit board files by
     9	   hand.
    10	5. Link the relevant `.spec/` documents to the task as precondition resources.
    11	6. Do not implement VPN work until the generated plan has explicit user approval.
    12	
    13	Changes that alter protocol, security, privacy, entitlements, routing, failure
    14	modes, or supported platforms must update the corresponding specification and
    15	`.spec/decisions.md`.
    16	
    17	## Development baseline
    18	
    19	The current application requires macOS 14+, Xcode 15.3+, and Swift 5.10.
    20	
    21	```sh
    22	make test
    23	make app
    24	```
    25	
    26	The planned multi-target VPN has additional gates in `.spec/validation.md`.
    27	Passing current SwiftPM tests does not validate the future Network Extension.
    28	
    29	## Commits and pull requests
    30	
    31	- Keep commits focused and do not mix generated build output with source/docs.
    32	- Use the configured Ivan Oparin identity and signed commits for this repository.
    33	- Never commit signing certificates, App Store Connect keys, SSH private keys,
    34	  passphrases, provisioning secrets, tokens, or captured user traffic.
    35	- Include board element IDs and human-readable names in implementation pull
    36	  request descriptions once execution begins.
    37	- Report verification commands and any untested physical-device requirement.
    38	- Do not add AI attribution or `Co-Authored-By` trailers.
    39	
    40	## Generated and local state
    41	
    42	`.agents/`, `.claude/`, `.codex/`, `.local/`, `.temp/`, build output, and local
    43	credentials are machine-local. `.task-board/`, `.planning/`, `.spec/`, and
    44	durable `.research/` evidence are source-controlled.

