--- destination current state ---
     1	# Current state and migration boundary
     2	
     3	Last verified: 2026-07-15.
     4	
     5	## What exists
     6	
     7	The repository builds one SwiftPM macOS 14 executable, `ReluxProxy`, packaged by
     8	shell scripts as a menu bar `.app` and DMG.
     9	
    10	The app:
    11	
    12	- stores SSH host, account, and local SOCKS port in `AppStorage`;
    13	- launches `/usr/bin/ssh -N -C -D 127.0.0.1:<port>` with fail-fast, keepalive,
    14	  timeout, batch-mode, and quiet logging options;
    15	- inherits host aliases, keys, ProxyJump, and other behavior from the user's
    16	  `~/.ssh/config`;
    17	- reports connecting, connected, disconnecting, disconnected, and failed states;
    18	- requires the user to configure a browser or other SOCKS-aware client manually;
    19	- intentionally runs outside the App Sandbox so it can spawn system SSH.
    20	
    21	Tests cover profile validation and exact SSH argument construction. CI runs
    22	`swift test` and builds an ad-hoc-signed universal application bundle.
    23	
    24	Tagged releases import the Relux Works Developer ID certificate, build a
    25	universal app, sign the DMG, notarize and staple it, validate it with Gatekeeper,
    26	publish checksums/provenance, and upload both versioned and stable
    27	`ReluxProxy.dmg` assets.
    28	
    29	## What does not exist
    30	
    31	The current app is not a system VPN and does not contain:
    32	
    33	- iOS targets;
    34	- `NETunnelProviderManager` or packet-tunnel extensions;
    35	- a userspace TCP/IP stack or packetFlow bridge;
    36	- an embedded SSH implementation;
    37	- UDP or system DNS forwarding;
    38	- the remote `relux-relay` executable/protocol;
    39	- shared profile/Keychain state between host and extension;
    40	- Network Extension entitlements or provisioning;
    41	- TestFlight/App Store release automation.
    42	
    43	## Migration boundary
    44	
    45	The planned product in `.spec/` is a new architecture, not an incremental toggle
    46	inside `TunnelController`. Existing source files remain the current SOCKS
    47	product until board-approved migration tasks create the multi-target project.
    48	
    49	During migration:
    50	
    51	- the stable macOS release path must remain reproducible;
    52	- current host/account defaults and user expectations need an explicit migration
    53	  decision;
    54	- the system-SSH implementation may remain as a macOS legacy mode, be replaced,
    55	  or be retired only through a product decision;
    56	- SwiftPM tests and release history must not be silently discarded;
    57	- Network Extension code must not be backported into the current manual app
    58	  without the architecture and entitlement gates in `.spec/validation.md`.
--- delivery ---
     1	# Delivery plan
     2	
     3	This document defines milestone intent. The canonical task order and dependency
     4	phases are generated from `.task-board/` and saved under `.planning/`.
     5	Implementation is not authorized by this planning branch.
     6	
     7	## Estimates
     8	
     9	The current engineering estimate is 23–36 working days for a production-capable
    10	baseline after architecture and entitlement gates, excluding uncertain App
    11	Review elapsed time. A failed SSH-engine candidate may add 3–4 days for the
    12	alternate integration. Estimates are planning ranges, not commitments.
    13	
    14	## M0 — viability spikes (3–5 days)
    15	
    16	- Resolve platform-intent and provisioning gates A0/P0.
    17	- Create the shared package/CLI harness and disposable physical-iPhone provider
    18	  shell needed for measurements.
    19	- Prove public packetFlow/socketpair/HEV bridge on device.
    20	- Benchmark MTU/buffering/memory and decide whether an HEV fork is justified.
    21	- Complete the NIOSSH-fork versus libssh2 matrix, including channel windows and
    22	  multi-gigabyte rekey, and record one engine decision.
    23	
    24	Exit: no unresolved stop-the-line platform, packet bridge, memory, or SSH-engine
    25	assumption remains for the baseline.
    26	
    27	## M1 — system VPN with TCP and safe DNS (5–8 days)
    28	
    29	- Establish the multi-target project and host/extension profile boundary.
    30	- Implement host-key verified public-key SSH on one lane.
    31	- Connect HEV SOCKS TCP to `direct-tcpip`.
    32	- Install IPv4/IPv6 routes and a leak-free DNS-over-TCP path.
    33	- Show system VPN state, profile fields, connect/disconnect, errors, and external
    34	  IP evidence on iOS and macOS.
    35	
    36	Exit: one `relux` profile provides stable full-device TCP and DNS on physical
    37	iPhone and Mac.
    38	
    39	## M2 — UDP relay and degraded mode (4–6 days)
    40	
    41	- Freeze relay v1 conformance vectors and portable build matrix.
    42	- Implement exec upload, verification, atomic install, stdio lifecycle, UDP
    43	  association/resource limits, and health.
    44	- Connect HEV UDP-in-TCP framing to the relay.
    45	- Provide full and degraded capability modes with DNS behavior in both.
    46	
    47	Exit: representative UDP works through the exit host, while every relay failure
    48	falls back safely to TCP + leak-free DNS or an explicit failure.
    49	
    50	## M3 — resilience and performance (7–10 days)
    51	
    52	- Add two-to-four lane pool, per-channel window policy, congestion-aware new-flow
    53	  assignment, and QUIC modes.
    54	- Implement physical-path endpoint resolution, route rebuilding, `reasserting`,
    55	  retry/backoff, and bounded transport overlap.
    56	- Cover Wi-Fi/cellular, NAT64, sleep/wake, captive network, kill-switch modes,
    57	  lane/rekey failures, and memory pressure.
    58	- Tune HEV/socket buffers/MTU only from measurements.
    59	
    60	Exit: the validation matrix passes without routing loops, ordinary DNS leaks,
    61	unbounded buffers, or avoidable extension termination.
    62	
    63	## M4 — product and release (4–7 days)
    64	
    65	- Complete profile/key/host-trust UX, accessibility, diagnostics, privacy
    66	  disclosure, support export, and settings.
    67	- Finish macOS signing/notarization and iOS archive/TestFlight pipelines.
    68	- Generate relay manifest, third-party notices, SBOM, checksums, stable macOS
    69	  asset, release notes, and App Review package.
    70	- Run release-candidate regression on the supported device/OS matrix.
    71	
    72	Exit: reviewed, signed release candidates install through intended channels and
    73	the App Review submission is complete.
    74	
    75	## Critical dependencies
    76	
    77	```text
    78	A0/P0 platform viability
    79	  -> M0 packet bridge + M0 SSH engine
    80	  -> M1 TCP/DNS system VPN
    81	  -> M2 relay/UDP/degraded mode
    82	  -> M3 resilience/lane pool
    83	  -> M4 distribution
    84	```
    85	
    86	UI/profile foundations that do not encode unproven transport assumptions may
    87	overlap late M0/M1. Release and privacy work starts early as specifications and
    88	CI scaffolding but cannot pass until behavior is stable.
    89	
    90	## Explicit deferrals
    91	
    92	ProxyJump/OpenSSH config import, password-only auth, shared exit services,
    93	Android/Windows clients, traffic obfuscation, fake DNS, selective per-app VPN,
    94	and advanced policy routing are not baseline milestones. They require separate
    95	product approval and board epics.

